\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\lquote Food is a significant focus of much of American literature\rquote . \par
\par
By comparing The Grapes of Wrath with at least one other text prescribed for this topic, discuss how far you agree with this view. \par
\par
  \par
\par
Given that my set texts,  The Great Gatsby (1925) and The Grapes of Wrath (1939) both fall towards the end of OCR\rquote s date range, 1880 \f1\endash  1940, and my wider reading text, Edith Wharton\rquote s Ethan Frome (1911) is also a twentieth century novel, it makes sense to limit the scope of this discussion to the twentieth century literature I have read, and not to generalise about the 1880 \endash  1900. With this in mind, yes, food is a significant focus of American literature. The Great Gatsby, set in the Roaring Twenties uses food (and drink) as a way to explore the hedonism and the conspicuous consumption of the era. In many ways, Steinbeck\rquote s novel could hardly be more different: the Joads\rquote  economic migration and the ruthless exploitation of land and food by banks and giant farms means that food is scarce. However, despite this, both writers present food as the bedrock of human society and the main characters in both are generous with it.   \par
